Before — `street`, `city`, `zip` travel together as three unrelated parameters, and nothing stops a caller from passing them in the wrong order:

```java
void shipOrder(String street, String city, String zip) {
    validateZip(zip);
    label.print(street, city, zip);
}

void billOrder(String street, String city, String zip) {
    validateZip(zip);
    invoice.setAddress(street + ", " + city + " " + zip);
}
```

After — the clump becomes a type, and validation happens once, at construction:

```java
record Address(String street, String city, String zip) {
    Address {
        validateZip(zip);
    }
    String formatted() {
        return street + ", " + city + " " + zip;
    }
}

void shipOrder(Address address) {
    label.print(address.formatted());
}

void billOrder(Address address) {
    invoice.setAddress(address.formatted());
}
```

The zip validation used to need remembering at every call site; now it's impossible to construct an invalid `Address` at all.
